IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `whsact-dates`

-- Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) -- the claims machinery a worker meets: a civil claim for discriminatory or
-- coercive conduct, compensation after a conviction, compensation from the Territory for an inspector's entry powers,
-- review of decisions, asking for a prosecution, the time for prosecutions, industrial manslaughter, and the penalties.
--
-- SEAT: the worker (or prospective worker, or the person whose commercial arrangement was ended) who was dismissed,
-- disadvantaged or coerced for raising or acting on work health and safety; the worker affected by an inspector's
-- notice; the worker, or the family of a worker who died, who wants the business prosecuted. "Can I take them to court
-- for sacking me over a safety complaint?", "By when?", "What can the court order?", "Can I ask for a review of the
-- inspector's notice, and by when?", "Can I make the regulator prosecute?", "How long have they got to prosecute?",
-- "Could my partner's death be industrial manslaughter?". The business sued answers the same questions; no second seat.
-- The regulator, the DPP, the inspector, the ACAT and the court get no seat.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts. No question takes a DEONTIC or an EVENT. Two duties with deadlines are regulative
-- rules run inside questions: the claimant's duty to commence a s 112 proceeding within 1 year (s 113 (1)), and the
-- regulator's duty to advise a person who asked for a prosecution within 3 months (s 231 (2)).
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: Part 6 (ss 104-108, 110-115; s 109 only as noted), s 184, Part 12 (ss 223, 224, 226, 228, 229),
-- Division 13.1 (ss 231, 232), Division 2.5 (ss 31-33 penalties) and Division 2.6 (ss 34A, 34B), Schedule 4 (penalty
-- amounts, indexation and rounding), s 261. What is out, and why: NOTES.md.
--
-- Edition: Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT), A2011-35, Republication No 30 (R30), effective 26 November 2025,
-- deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/A2011-35.txt.
--
-- (c) Australian Capital Territory. Used under CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md). Unofficial text.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Judgments the Act hands to a court, the regulator or the ACAT are
-- INPUTS. "(the text's no)" and "NOTHING-kind" follow the rulings on negatives (NOTES.md).

§ `Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT): the claims machinery`

§§ `Part 6 -- Discriminatory and coercive conduct (this group arises only if you were disadvantaged or pressured over work health and safety)`

-- s 105 (1) and (2). Each kind of conduct also counts when someone organises it, or threatens to organise or take it.
DECLARE `What was done to me` IS ONE OF
    `I was dismissed`
    `my contract for services was terminated`
    `I was put to my detriment in my engagement`
    `my position was altered to my detriment`
    `I was refused, or not offered, an engagement I sought`
    `I was treated less favourably than another prospective worker would be in the terms of engagement offered`
    `a commercial arrangement with me was terminated`
    `someone refused or failed to enter into a commercial arrangement with me`
    `none of these things was done`

-- s 105: "a person engages in discriminatory conduct if" -- the text's no when no limb holds.
@export Is what was done to me "discriminatory conduct" under the Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) (s 105)? It includes dismissing a worker, ending their contract, putting them to their detriment or altering their position to their detriment, refusing to engage or offering worse terms to a prospective worker, and ending or refusing a commercial arrangement, or organising or threatening any of these.
GIVEN `what was done` IS A `What was done to me` @desc What was done to you (or organised, or threatened)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 105 -- is it discriminatory conduct` `what was done` IF
    NOT (`what was done` EQUALS `none of these things was done`)

-- s 106 (a) to (j): the circumstances that make conduct "for a prohibited reason" when it is engaged in because of them.
DECLARE `My circumstance` IS ONE OF
    `I am, was or propose to be a health and safety representative or committee member`
    `I undertake, undertook or propose to undertake another role under the Act`
    `I exercise or perform, exercised or performed, or propose to exercise or perform, a power or function as a health and safety representative or committee member`
    `I exercise, exercised or propose to exercise a power under the Act, or in a particular way`
    `I perform, performed or propose to perform a function under the Act, or in a particular way`
    `I refrain, refrained or propose to refrain from exercising a power or performing a function under the Act, or in a particular way`
    `I assist, assisted or propose to assist, or give, gave or propose to give information to, a person exercising a power or performing a function under the Act`
    `I raise, raised or propose to raise a work health and safety issue or concern with the business, an inspector, a WHS entry permit-holder, a health and safety representative or committee member, another worker, or another person with a duty, power or function under the Act`
    `I am, was or propose to be involved in resolving a work health and safety issue under the Act`
    `I am taking, took or propose to take action to seek compliance by any person with a duty or obligation under the Act`
    `none of these is true of me`

-- s 106: "Conduct ... is engaged in for a prohibited reason if it is engaged in because" of a listed circumstance.
@export Was the conduct against me engaged in "for a prohibited reason" (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) s 106)? It is, if it was done because you held a health and safety role, used a power or function under the Act, helped someone who did, raised a safety concern, helped resolve a safety issue, or sought compliance with the Act.
GIVEN `the circumstance` IS A `My circumstance` @desc Which of these is true of you (choose the one the conduct is said to be because of)
      `because of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct was engaged in because of that circumstance
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 106 -- was it for a prohibited reason` `the circumstance` `because of it` IF
        (NOT (`the circumstance` EQUALS `none of these is true of me`))
    AND `because of it`

-- s 112 (2): the three kinds of conduct for which the court may make orders.
DECLARE `The conduct I complain of` IS ONE OF
    `the person I sue engaged in discriminatory conduct against me (s 112 (2) (a))`
    `the person I sue requested, instructed, induced, encouraged, authorised or assisted someone else to engage in discriminatory conduct against me (s 112 (2) (b))`
    `the person I sue coerced or induced, or tried to, about powers, functions or roles under the Act (s 108, s 112 (2) (c))`

-- s 112 (6): "each of the following is an eligible person: (a) a person affected by the contravention; (b) a person
-- authorised as a representative by" such a person. The list is not closed with "only": a person outside it gets
-- NOTHING-kind (fork F6).
DECLARE `Who is applying` IS ONE OF
    `I am the person affected by the contravention`
    `I am a representative authorised by the person affected`
    `neither`

DECLARE `Civil proceeding` IS ONE OF
    `the court may make orders against the person (s 112 (2)); see the question on which orders`
    `no: the conduct was not discriminatory conduct for a prohibited reason that was a substantial reason for it (s 112 (2), (4)) (the text's no)`
    `no: the person sued proved, on the balance of probabilities, that the prohibited reason was not a substantial reason (s 113 (2)) (the text's no)`
    `no: the person sued proved the defence that the conduct was reasonable and a substantial reason for it was to comply with the WHS laws (s 113 (3)) (the text's no)`
    `no: there was no coercion or inducement contrary to s 108 (s 112 (2) (c)) (the text's no)`
    `no: a reasonable direction by a police officer or emergency services worker in an emergency is not coercion (s 108 (3)) (the text's no)`
    `no: a s 111 order was already made in a prosecution for the same conduct (s 114 (3)) (the text's no)`
    `s 112 lets an affected person or their authorised representative apply; it says nothing of others (NOTHING-kind)`

-- s 112 (4): "a person may be found to have engaged in discriminatory conduct for a prohibited reason only if a reason
-- referred to in section 106 was a substantial reason for the conduct". s 113 (2): if a prohibited reason is alleged,
-- "that reason is presumed to be a substantial reason ... unless the defendant proves, on the balance of
-- probabilities, that the reason was not a substantial reason". s 113 (3): "It is a defence ... if the defendant
-- proves that (a) the conduct was reasonable in the circumstances; and (b) a substantial reason for the conduct was to
-- comply with the requirements of this Act or a corresponding WHS law." The proofs are the court's findings: asked.
-- s 108 (1) and (3): coercion; "a reasonable direction given by an emergency services worker in an emergency is not an
-- action with intent to coerce or induce".
@export Can the court make orders against the person who discriminated against me or coerced me over work health and safety (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) s 112, s 113)? A prohibited reason you allege is presumed to be a substantial reason unless they prove otherwise, and they have a defence if they prove the conduct was reasonable and a substantial reason for it was to comply with the WHS laws.
GIVEN `who` IS A `Who is applying` @desc Whether you are the person affected by the conduct, or a representative they authorised
      `the conduct` IS A `The conduct I complain of` @desc What the person you are suing did
      `what was done` IS A `What was done to me` @desc For discriminatory conduct: what was done to you (or organised, or threatened)
      `the circumstance` IS A `My circumstance` @desc For discriminatory conduct: the circumstance you say it was done because of
      `alleged` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For discriminatory conduct: whether you allege it was done because of that circumstance
      `rebutted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For discriminatory conduct: whether the person sued proves, on the balance of probabilities, that this reason was not a substantial reason for the conduct (answer as the court would find)
      `conduct reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For discriminatory conduct: whether the person sued proves the conduct was reasonable in the circumstances (answer as the court would find)
      `to comply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For discriminatory conduct: whether the person sued proves a substantial reason for it was to comply with the WHS Act or a corresponding WHS law (answer as the court would find)
      `coerced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For coercion: whether they organised or took, or threatened to organise or take, action against you or a third person (including not taking an action) intending to make you use or not use a power or function under the Act, use it a particular way, or give up a role under it
      `emergency direction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For coercion: whether what was done was a reasonable direction by a police officer or a member of an emergency service in an emergency
      `s 111 order made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a court has already ordered compensation or reinstatement for you in a prosecution for the same conduct (s 111)
GIVETH A `Civil proceeding`
`s 112 -- can the court make orders for me` `who` `the conduct` `what was done` `the circumstance` `alleged` `rebutted` `conduct reasonable` `to comply` `coerced` `emergency direction` `s 111 order made` MEANS
    IF `who` EQUALS `neither`
    THEN `s 112 lets an affected person or their authorised representative apply; it says nothing of others (NOTHING-kind)`
    ELSE IF `the conduct` EQUALS `the person I sue coerced or induced, or tried to, about powers, functions or roles under the Act (s 108, s 112 (2) (c))`
         THEN IF `emergency direction`
              THEN `no: a reasonable direction by a police officer or emergency services worker in an emergency is not coercion (s 108 (3)) (the text's no)`
              ELSE IF `coerced`
                   THEN `the court may make orders against the person (s 112 (2)); see the question on which orders`
                   ELSE `no: there was no coercion or inducement contrary to s 108 (s 112 (2) (c)) (the text's no)`
         ELSE IF `s 111 order made`
              THEN `no: a s 111 order was already made in a prosecution for the same conduct (s 114 (3)) (the text's no)`
              ELSE IF NOT (`s 105 -- is it discriminatory conduct` `what was done` AND `s 106 -- was it for a prohibited reason` `the circumstance` `alleged`)
                   THEN `no: the conduct was not discriminatory conduct for a prohibited reason that was a substantial reason for it (s 112 (2), (4)) (the text's no)`
                   ELSE IF `rebutted`
                        THEN `no: the person sued proved, on the balance of probabilities, that the prohibited reason was not a substantial reason (s 113 (2)) (the text's no)`
                        ELSE IF `conduct reasonable` AND `to comply`
                             THEN `no: the person sued proved the defence that the conduct was reasonable and a substantial reason for it was to comply with the WHS laws (s 113 (3)) (the text's no)`
                             ELSE `the court may make orders against the person (s 112 (2)); see the question on which orders`

-- s 112 (3) and s 115 (b).
DECLARE `Order` IS ONE OF
    `an injunction, from the Supreme Court (s 112 (1) (a), (3) (a))`
    `compensation the court considers appropriate, to be paid within a period the court specifies (s 112 (3) (b))`
    `reinstatement or re-employment in your former position or, if it is not available, a similar position (s 112 (3) (c) (i))`
    `employment in the position you applied for or a similar position (s 112 (3) (c) (ii))`
    `any other order the court considers appropriate (s 112 (3) (d))`
    `compensation in the criminal proceeding, to be paid within a period the court specifies (s 111 (a))`
    `reinstatement or re-employment in your former position or a similar position, in the criminal proceeding (s 111 (b) (i))`
    `employment in the position you applied for or a similar position, in the criminal proceeding (s 111 (b) (ii))`

-- s 112 (3) (c) and s 111 (b): "in relation to a worker who was or is an employee or prospective employee".
DECLARE `My engagement` IS ONE OF
    `I am, or was, an employee`
    `I am a prospective employee`
    `I am, or was, a worker other than an employee (for example a contractor or volunteer)`
    `I am not a worker (for example, my commercial arrangement was ended)`

-- s 112 (3): (a) an injunction (from the Supreme Court, s 112 (1) (a)); (b) compensation "for conduct referred to in
-- subsection (2) (a) or (b)"; (c) reinstatement or employment "for conduct referred to in subsection (2) (a) in relation
-- to a worker who was or is an employee or prospective employee"; (d) any other order. s 115 (b): a person cannot
-- "recover any compensation under division 6.3 if the person has received compensation for the matter under a law of the
-- Commonwealth or a State".
@export Which orders can the court make for me in a civil proceeding for discriminatory or coercive conduct (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) s 112 (3), s 115 (b))? Ask this once the court may make orders against the person.
GIVEN `the conduct` IS A `The conduct I complain of` @desc What the person you are suing did
      `my engagement` IS A `My engagement` @desc Whether you are or were an employee, a prospective employee, another kind of worker, or not a worker
      `compensated elsewhere` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already received compensation for the matter under a law of the Commonwealth or a State
GIVETH A LIST OF `Order`
`s 112 (3) -- which orders may the court make for me` `the conduct` `my engagement` `compensated elsewhere` MEANS
    IF `the conduct` EQUALS `the person I sue coerced or induced, or tried to, about powers, functions or roles under the Act (s 108, s 112 (2) (c))`
    THEN LIST `an injunction, from the Supreme Court (s 112 (1) (a), (3) (a))`, `any other order the court considers appropriate (s 112 (3) (d))`
    ELSE `an injunction, from the Supreme Court (s 112 (1) (a), (3) (a))` FOLLOWED BY (append `compensation` (append `employment` (LIST `any other order the court considers appropriate (s 112 (3) (d))`)))
    WHERE
        `compensation` MEANS
            IF `compensated elsewhere`
            THEN EMPTY
            ELSE LIST `compensation the court considers appropriate, to be paid within a period the court specifies (s 112 (3) (b))`
        `employment` MEANS
            IF `the conduct` EQUALS `the person I sue engaged in discriminatory conduct against me (s 112 (2) (a))`
            THEN IF `my engagement` EQUALS `I am, or was, an employee`
                 THEN LIST `reinstatement or re-employment in your former position or, if it is not available, a similar position (s 112 (3) (c) (i))`
                 ELSE IF `my engagement` EQUALS `I am a prospective employee`
                      THEN LIST `employment in the position you applied for or a similar position (s 112 (3) (c) (ii))`
                      ELSE EMPTY
            ELSE EMPTY

-- s 115 (a) and (c). (b) is in the orders question above.
DECLARE `Another proceeding` IS ONE OF
    `I have no other proceeding, application or complaint about the same matter under a Commonwealth or State law`
    `I commenced one under a Commonwealth or State law and it has not been withdrawn`
    `I commenced one under a Commonwealth or State law and withdrew it`
    `I failed in one under a Commonwealth or State law, not about workers compensation`
    `I failed in one under a Commonwealth or State law about workers compensation`

DECLARE `Multiple actions` IS ONE OF
    `s 115 does not stop you on these facts`
    `you cannot commence the proceeding while your proceeding, application or complaint about the same matter under a Commonwealth or State law has not been withdrawn (s 115 (a)) (the text's no)`
    `you cannot commence or continue the proceeding, because you failed in a proceeding, application or complaint about the same matter under a Commonwealth or State law (s 115 (c)) (the text's no)`

-- s 115: "A person cannot (a) commence a proceeding under division 6.3 if the person has commenced a proceeding or made
-- an application or complaint in relation to the same matter under a law of the Commonwealth or a State and that
-- proceeding, application or complaint has not been withdrawn; ... (c) commence or continue an application under
-- division 6.3 if the person has failed in a proceeding, application or complaint in relation to the same matter under a
-- law of the Commonwealth or a State, other than a proceeding, application or complaint relating to workers'
-- compensation." Where neither "if" holds, s 115 does not bar (its own conditions fail).
@export Does another claim I made about the same matter stop me bringing a civil proceeding for discriminatory or coercive conduct (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) s 115)? A pending (not withdrawn) Commonwealth or State claim, or a failed one other than for workers compensation, stops you.
GIVEN `the other` IS AN `Another proceeding` @desc Whether you have made another claim about the same matter under a Commonwealth or State law, and what became of it
GIVETH A `Multiple actions`
`s 115 -- does another claim stop me` `the other` MEANS
    IF `the other` EQUALS `I commenced one under a Commonwealth or State law and it has not been withdrawn`
    THEN `you cannot commence the proceeding while your proceeding, application or complaint about the same matter under a Commonwealth or State law has not been withdrawn (s 115 (a)) (the text's no)`
    ELSE IF `the other` EQUALS `I failed in one under a Commonwealth or State law, not about workers compensation`
         THEN `you cannot commence or continue the proceeding, because you failed in a proceeding, application or complaint about the same matter under a Commonwealth or State law (s 115 (c)) (the text's no)`
         ELSE `s 115 does not stop you on these facts`

§§ `s 113 (1) -- The time to commence a civil proceeding`

-- s 113 (1): "A proceeding brought under section 112 must be commenced not more than 1 year after the date on which the
-- applicant knew or ought to have known that the cause of action accrued." The date is the claimant's fact (when they
-- "ought to have known" is a judgment, asked as the date a court would find). Counted in days from that date.
DECLARE `Applicant` IS ONE OF `the applicant`
DECLARE `Applicant step` IS ONE OF `commence the s 112 proceeding`

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Applicant` `Applicant step`
`commence within 1 year` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the applicant`
    MUST `commence the s 112 proceeding`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the applicant` BECAUSE "a s 112 proceeding must be commenced not more than 1 year after the date the applicant knew or ought to have known that the cause of action accrued (s 113 (1))"

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER, `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `commencing on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`commence within 1 year` `the days allowed`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `the applicant` `commence the s 112 proceeding` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

@export By when must I commence my civil proceeding for discriminatory or coercive conduct (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) s 113 (1))? Not more than 1 year after the date you knew, or ought to have known, that the cause of action accrued; this gives that last day.
GIVEN `knew on` IS A DATE @desc The date you knew, or ought to have known (as a court would find), that your cause of action accrued (usually the date of the dismissal or other conduct)
GIVETH A DATE
`s 113 (1) -- the last day to commence` `knew on` MEANS 1 `years after` `knew on`

DECLARE `Commenced in time` IS ONE OF
    `in time: commenced not more than 1 year after you knew or ought to have known the cause of action accrued (s 113 (1))`
    `not yet commenced, and still in time (s 113 (1))`
    `out of time: more than 1 year after you knew or ought to have known the cause of action accrued (s 113 (1)) (the text's no)`

@export Am I, or was I, in time to commence a civil proceeding for discriminatory or coercive conduct (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) s 113 (1))?
GIVEN `knew on` IS A DATE @desc The date you knew, or ought to have known (as a court would find), that your cause of action accrued
      `commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have commenced the proceeding
      `commenced on` IS A DATE @desc The date you commenced it (if you have not, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (used when you have not commenced)
GIVETH A `Commenced in time`
`s 113 (1) -- am I in time` `knew on` `commenced` `commenced on` `today` MEANS
    IF `commenced`
    THEN IF `commencing on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day commenced`
         THEN `in time: commenced not more than 1 year after you knew or ought to have known the cause of action accrued (s 113 (1))`
         ELSE `out of time: more than 1 year after you knew or ought to have known the cause of action accrued (s 113 (1)) (the text's no)`
    ELSE IF `the last day` `falls before` `today`
         THEN `out of time: more than 1 year after you knew or ought to have known the cause of action accrued (s 113 (1)) (the text's no)`
         ELSE `not yet commenced, and still in time (s 113 (1))`
    WHERE
        `the last day` MEANS 1 `years after` `knew on`
        `the days allowed` MEANS `the days from` `knew on` `to` `the last day`
        -- A proceeding commenced before the date of knowledge is counted as commenced on that date (fork F5).
        `the raw day` MEANS `the days from` `knew on` `to` `commenced on`
        `the day commenced` MEANS IF `the raw day` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `the raw day`

§§ `s 111 and s 114 -- Compensation or reinstatement after a conviction (this group arises only if someone was prosecuted for discriminatory conduct against you)`

-- s 111: "If a person is convicted or found guilty of an offence against section 104 ... or section 107 ..., the court may
-- (in addition to imposing a penalty) make either or both of the following orders: (a) ... compensation to the person
-- who was the subject of the discriminatory conduct ...; (b) in relation to a person who was or is an employee or
-- prospective employee, an order that (i) the person be reinstated or re-employed ... or (ii) the person be employed in
-- the position for which he or she had applied". s 114 (2): "If the court makes an order under section 112 ... the court
-- cannot make an order under section 111" for the same conduct.
@export After the person who discriminated against me is convicted, which orders can the criminal court make for me (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) s 111, s 114 (2))? None, if a civil court already made a s 112 order for the same conduct.
GIVEN `convicted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person has been convicted or found guilty of an offence of discriminatory conduct (s 104) or of requesting, inducing or assisting it (s 107)
      `the subject` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were the person who was the subject of the discriminatory conduct
      `my engagement` IS A `My engagement` @desc Whether you are or were an employee, a prospective employee, another kind of worker, or not a worker
      `s 112 order made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a court has already made an order under s 112 (civil proceeding) for the same conduct
GIVETH A LIST OF `Order`
`s 111 -- which orders may the criminal court make for me` `convicted` `the subject` `my engagement` `s 112 order made` MEANS
    IF (NOT `convicted`) OR (NOT `the subject`) OR `s 112 order made`
    THEN EMPTY
    ELSE `compensation in the criminal proceeding, to be paid within a period the court specifies (s 111 (a))` FOLLOWED BY `employment`
    WHERE
        `employment` MEANS
            IF `my engagement` EQUALS `I am, or was, an employee`
            THEN LIST `reinstatement or re-employment in your former position or a similar position, in the criminal proceeding (s 111 (b) (i))`
            ELSE IF `my engagement` EQUALS `I am a prospective employee`
                 THEN LIST `employment in the position you applied for or a similar position, in the criminal proceeding (s 111 (b) (ii))`
                 ELSE EMPTY

§§ `Division 9.4 -- Compensation from the Territory (this group arises only if an inspector entered or searched a place)`

-- s 184 (1): "A person may claim compensation from the Territory if the person incurs loss or expense because of the
-- exercise, or purported exercise, of a power under division 9.3"; (3): "The court may order compensation to be paid
-- only if it is satisfied it is just to make the order". The regulation of s 184 (4) is not deposited.
DECLARE `Compensation from the Territory` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may claim compensation from the Territory, in a court of competent jurisdiction or in a prosecution brought against you under this Act, and the court may order it (s 184)`
    `you may claim (s 184 (1)), but the court may not order compensation, because it is not satisfied it is just (s 184 (3)) (the text's no)`
    `no: s 184 gives no claim, as your loss or expense was not caused by an inspector's exercise of an entry power (the text's no: its "if" fails)`

@export Can I get compensation from the Territory for loss or expense caused by an inspector's entry, search or seizure powers (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) s 184)?
GIVEN `caused by entry powers` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you incurred loss or expense because an inspector exercised, or purported to exercise, a power relating to entry (entry, search, warrants, documents, seizure)
      `just` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied it is just to order compensation in the circumstances of your case (answer as the court would find)
GIVETH A `Compensation from the Territory`
`s 184 -- can I get compensation from the Territory` `caused by entry powers` `just` MEANS
    IF NOT `caused by entry powers`
    THEN `no: s 184 gives no claim, as your loss or expense was not caused by an inspector's exercise of an entry power (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
    ELSE IF `just`
         THEN `yes: you may claim compensation from the Territory, in a court of competent jurisdiction or in a prosecution brought against you under this Act, and the court may order it (s 184)`
         ELSE `you may claim (s 184 (1)), but the court may not order compensation, because it is not satisfied it is just (s 184 (3)) (the text's no)`

§§ `Part 12 -- Review of decisions (this group arises only if an inspector or the regulator made a decision that affects you)`

-- Table 223: the decisions of which "a worker whose interests are affected by the decision" is an eligible person
-- (items 1, 3, 4, 9, 9A, 9B, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14), the seizure items (7, 8; "the person entitled to the thing"), and
-- the rest (items 2, 2A, 3A, 5, 6, 15), for which a worker as such is not an eligible person.
DECLARE `The decision` IS ONE OF
    `a decision following failure to commence negotiations about work groups (s 54 (2))`
    `a decision about a health and safety committee (s 76 (6))`
    `an inspector's decision on review of a provisional improvement notice (s 102)`
    `the issue of an improvement notice (s 191)`
    `the issue of a prohibited asbestos notice (s 197B)`
    `an extension of time to comply with a prohibited asbestos notice (s 197E)`
    `an extension of time to comply with an improvement notice (s 194)`
    `the issue of a prohibition notice (s 195)`
    `the issue of a non-disturbance notice (s 198)`
    `the issue of a subsequent notice (s 201)`
    `the regulator's decision to vary or cancel a notice (s 207)`
    `the forfeiture of a seized thing (s 179)`
    `a decision about returning a seized thing (s 180)`
    `another decision (training, entry permits, or under the Regulation)`

-- s 223 (1) (b): Table 223 sets out "who is eligible to apply for review". The table names the eligible persons for each
-- decision: read as closed (fork F7).
DECLARE `Can I seek review` IS ONE OF
    `yes: as a worker whose interests are affected by the decision, you are an eligible person (s 223, table 223)`
    `yes: as the person entitled to the seized thing, you are an eligible person (s 223, table 223)`
    `no: table 223 does not make you an eligible person for this decision (the text's no)`
    `table 223 makes a worker an eligible person only for the decisions it names; this one is not encoded as one, and may be prescribed by the Regulation (item 15, not deposited) (NOTHING-kind)`

GIVEN `the decision` IS A `The decision`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a worker is eligible for` `the decision` IF
        (NOT (`the decision` EQUALS `the forfeiture of a seized thing (s 179)`))
    AND (NOT (`the decision` EQUALS `a decision about returning a seized thing (s 180)`))
    AND (NOT (`the decision` EQUALS `another decision (training, entry permits, or under the Regulation)`))

@export Can I, as a worker, apply for review of this decision (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) s 223 and table 223)? A worker whose interests are affected may seek review of decisions such as the issue of an improvement, prohibition or non-disturbance notice and an inspector's decision on a provisional improvement notice.
GIVEN `the decision` IS A `The decision` @desc Which decision you want reviewed
      `affected` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a worker whose interests are affected by the decision
      `entitled to the thing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a seized thing: whether you are the person it was seized from (and are entitled to possess it), or its owner where that person is not
GIVETH A `Can I seek review`
`s 223 -- can I apply for review` `the decision` `affected` `entitled to the thing` MEANS
    IF `the decision` EQUALS `another decision (training, entry permits, or under the Regulation)`
    THEN `table 223 makes a worker an eligible person only for the decisions it names; this one is not encoded as one, and may be prescribed by the Regulation (item 15, not deposited) (NOTHING-kind)`
    ELSE IF `a worker is eligible for` `the decision`
         THEN IF `affected`
              THEN `yes: as a worker whose interests are affected by the decision, you are an eligible person (s 223, table 223)`
              ELSE `no: table 223 does not make you an eligible person for this decision (the text's no)`
         ELSE IF `entitled to the thing`
              THEN `yes: as the person entitled to the seized thing, you are an eligible person (s 223, table 223)`
              ELSE `no: table 223 does not make you an eligible person for this decision (the text's no)`

-- s 224 (1): an eligible person may apply for internal review of a reviewable decision "other than a decision made by
-- the regulator or a delegate of the regulator" within "the relevant time after the day on which the decision first came
-- to the eligible person's notice; or such longer period that the regulator allows"; (3) relevant time: for a decision
-- to issue an improvement notice, "the period specified in the notice for compliance with the notice or 14 days,
-- whichever is the lesser"; otherwise 14 days.
-- s 229 (1): external review by the ACAT of a reviewable decision made by the regulator, or a decision made (or taken to
-- have been made) on internal review; (2): within 28 days for a decision to forfeit a thing, otherwise 14 days, after the
-- day the decision first came to the applicant's notice; or, if the ACAT required the regulator to give a statement of
-- reasons, within 14 days after the day the statement is provided.
DECLARE `Which review` IS ONE OF
    `the decision, not made by the regulator or a delegate of the regulator`
    `the decision, made by the regulator or a delegate of the regulator`
    `the decision made (or taken to have been made) on internal review`

DECLARE `Review route` IS ONE OF
    `internal review: apply to the regulator (s 224)`
    `external review: apply to the ACAT (s 229)`

@export Which review do I apply for, and to whom (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) s 224, s 229)? A decision not made by the regulator or its delegate goes first to internal review by the regulator; a decision of the regulator, or the decision on internal review, goes to the ACAT.
GIVEN `which` IS A `Which review` @desc Which decision you are seeking review of, and who made it
GIVETH A `Review route`
`s 224 and s 229 -- which review do I apply for` `which` MEANS
    IF `which` EQUALS `the decision, not made by the regulator or a delegate of the regulator`
    THEN `internal review: apply to the regulator (s 224)`
    ELSE `external review: apply to the ACAT (s 229)`

@export By when must I apply for review of a decision (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) s 224, s 229)? Internal review: 14 days after the decision first came to your notice (for an improvement notice, the compliance period if shorter), or a longer period the regulator allows. ACAT review: 14 days (28 for a forfeiture), or 14 days after a statement of reasons the ACAT required.
GIVEN `which` IS A `Which review` @desc Which decision you are seeking review of, and who made it
      `the decision` IS A `The decision` @desc What the original decision was
      `noticed on` IS A DATE @desc The day the decision first came to your notice
      `compliance days` IS A NUMBER @desc For the issue of an improvement notice: the number of days the notice gives for compliance (otherwise any number; it is not used)
      `longer allowed` IS A NUMBER @desc For internal review: the number of days the regulator has allowed you, if it allowed a longer period (0 if not)
      `reasons required` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For ACAT review: whether the ACAT has required the regulator to give you a statement of reasons
      `reasons given on` IS A DATE @desc The day the statement of reasons was provided (if none, any date; it is not used)
GIVETH A DATE
`s 224 and s 229 -- the last day to apply for review` `which` `the decision` `noticed on` `compliance days` `longer allowed` `reasons required` `reasons given on` MEANS
    IF `which` EQUALS `the decision, not made by the regulator or a delegate of the regulator`
    THEN `the internal days` `days after` `noticed on`
    ELSE IF `reasons required`
         THEN 14 `days after` `reasons given on`
         ELSE IF `the decision` EQUALS `the forfeiture of a seized thing (s 179)`
              THEN 28 `days after` `noticed on`
              ELSE 14 `days after` `noticed on`
    WHERE
        `the relevant time` MEANS
            IF `the decision` EQUALS `the issue of an improvement notice (s 191)` AND `compliance days` LESS THAN 14
            THEN `compliance days`
            ELSE 14
        `the internal days` MEANS
            IF `longer allowed` GREATER THAN `the relevant time` THEN `longer allowed` ELSE `the relevant time`

-- s 228 (1): "An application for an internal review of a reviewable decision (other than a decision to issue a
-- prohibition notice or a non-disturbance notice) stays the operation of the decision." (2): for those two, "the reviewer
-- may stay"; (4) and (5): the reviewer must decide an application for a stay within 1 working day, and if it has not,
-- "is taken to have made a decision to grant a stay".
DECLARE `Stay` IS ONE OF
    `stayed: your application for internal review stays the decision (s 228 (1))`
    `stayed: the reviewer decided to stay it (s 228 (2))`
    `stayed: the reviewer did not decide your application for a stay within 1 working day, so is taken to have granted it (s 228 (5))`
    `not stayed: the reviewer refused your application for a stay within 1 working day (s 228 (2), (4))`
    `not stayed unless the reviewer stays it on its own initiative (s 228 (2), (3)); you may apply for a stay`

@export Does applying for internal review stop the decision taking effect in the meantime (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) s 228)? It does, except for a prohibition notice or a non-disturbance notice, which the reviewer may stay; a stay application not decided within 1 working day is taken to be granted.
GIVEN `the decision` IS A `The decision` @desc What the decision under internal review is
      `stay asked` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a prohibition or non-disturbance notice: whether you applied to the reviewer for a stay
      `decided within a working day` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the reviewer decided your stay application within 1 working day of receiving it
      `stay granted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the reviewer decided to stay the decision (on your application or on its own initiative)
GIVETH A `Stay`
`s 228 -- is the decision stayed while it is reviewed` `the decision` `stay asked` `decided within a working day` `stay granted` MEANS
    IF NOT (`the decision` EQUALS `the issue of a prohibition notice (s 195)` OR `the decision` EQUALS `the issue of a non-disturbance notice (s 198)`)
    THEN `stayed: your application for internal review stays the decision (s 228 (1))`
    ELSE IF `stay asked` AND (NOT `decided within a working day`)
         THEN `stayed: the reviewer did not decide your application for a stay within 1 working day, so is taken to have granted it (s 228 (5))`
         ELSE IF `stay granted`
              THEN `stayed: the reviewer decided to stay it (s 228 (2))`
              ELSE IF `stay asked`
                   THEN `not stayed: the reviewer refused your application for a stay within 1 working day (s 228 (2), (4))`
                   ELSE `not stayed unless the reviewer stays it on its own initiative (s 228 (2), (3)); you may apply for a stay`

§§ `s 231 -- Asking for a prosecution (this group arises only if you think a serious offence was committed and no one has been prosecuted)`

-- s 231 (1): applies if a person "(i) reasonably considers that the occurrence of an act, matter or thing constitutes a
-- category 1 offence or a category 2 offence; or (ii) reasonably considers, from a coronial report or a proceeding at a
-- coronial inquiry or inquest, that a category 1 offence or a category 2 offence has been committed; and (b) no
-- prosecution for the offence has been brought". (1B): the request may be made "(a) if subsection (1) (a) (i) applies -- at
-- least 6 months but not more than 18 months after the act, matter or thing happens; or (b) if subsection (1) (a) (ii)
-- applies -- within 6 months after the day the report is made or the inquiry or inquest ends."
DECLARE `Why I think an offence was committed` IS ONE OF
    `from what happened: I reasonably consider it was a category 1 or category 2 offence`
    `from a coronial report, inquiry or inquest: I reasonably consider a category 1 or category 2 offence was committed`
    `neither of these`

DECLARE `My prosecution request` IS ONE OF
    `in time: you may make the written request to the regulator on this day (s 231 (1A), (1B))`
    `too early: a request on what happened may be made only from 6 months after it happened (s 231 (1B) (a)) (the text's no)`
    `too late: the time for the request has passed (s 231 (1B)) (the text's no)`
    `s 231 does not apply: a prosecution has been brought (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
    `s 231 does not apply: you do not reasonably consider a category 1 or category 2 offence was committed (the text's no: its "if" fails)`

@export May I ask the regulator in writing to bring a prosecution, on the day I plan to (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) s 231)? If no one has been prosecuted for what you reasonably consider a category 1 or 2 offence, you may ask from 6 to 18 months after it happened, or within 6 months after a coronial report or the end of an inquest that shows it.
GIVEN `why` IS A `Why I think an offence was committed` @desc On what you base your view that a category 1 or category 2 offence (a serious breach of a health and safety duty) was committed
      `prosecuted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a prosecution for the offence has already been brought
      `happened on` IS A DATE @desc The day the act, matter or thing (or failure) happened
      `report on` IS A DATE @desc For a coronial report or inquest: the day the report was made or the inquiry or inquest ended (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `request on` IS A DATE @desc The day you make, or plan to make, the written request
GIVETH A `My prosecution request`
`s 231 -- may I ask for a prosecution on this day` `why` `prosecuted` `happened on` `report on` `request on` MEANS
    IF `why` EQUALS `neither of these`
    THEN `s 231 does not apply: you do not reasonably consider a category 1 or category 2 offence was committed (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
    ELSE IF `prosecuted`
         THEN `s 231 does not apply: a prosecution has been brought (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
         ELSE IF `why` EQUALS `from what happened: I reasonably consider it was a category 1 or category 2 offence`
              THEN IF `request on` `falls before` (6 `months after` `happened on`)
                   THEN `too early: a request on what happened may be made only from 6 months after it happened (s 231 (1B) (a)) (the text's no)`
                   ELSE IF (18 `months after` `happened on`) `falls before` `request on`
                        THEN `too late: the time for the request has passed (s 231 (1B)) (the text's no)`
                        ELSE `in time: you may make the written request to the regulator on this day (s 231 (1A), (1B))`
              ELSE IF (6 `months after` `report on`) `falls before` `request on`
                   THEN `too late: the time for the request has passed (s 231 (1B)) (the text's no)`
                   ELSE `in time: you may make the written request to the regulator on this day (s 231 (1A), (1B))`

-- s 231 (2): "Within 3 months after the regulator receives a request the regulator must (a) advise the applicant (in
-- writing) whether the investigation is complete" and, if complete, whether it will refer the matter to the DPP or why
-- not. The regulator's duty, encoded for what the person must be given and by when. Counted in days to the day 3 months
-- after receipt.
DECLARE `Regulator` IS ONE OF `the regulator`
DECLARE `Regulator step` IS ONE OF `advise the applicant in writing`

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Regulator` `Regulator step`
`advise within 3 months` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the regulator`
    MUST `advise the applicant in writing`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the regulator` BECAUSE "no written advice within 3 months after receiving the request for a prosecution (s 231 (2))"

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER, `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `advice on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`advise within 3 months` `the days allowed`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `the regulator` `advise the applicant in writing` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

@export By when must the regulator answer my written request for a prosecution (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) s 231 (2))? Within 3 months after it receives the request, the regulator must tell you in writing whether the investigation is complete and, if it is, whether it has referred or will refer the matter to the DPP or why not.
GIVEN `received on` IS A DATE @desc The day the regulator received your request
GIVETH A DATE
`s 231 (2) -- the last day for the regulator's answer` `received on` MEANS 3 `months after` `received on`

DECLARE `The regulator's answer` IS ONE OF
    `in time: the regulator advised you in writing within 3 months after receiving your request (s 231 (2))`
    `not yet advised, and the 3 months have not run out (s 231 (2))`
    `late: the regulator did not advise you in writing within 3 months after receiving your request (s 231 (2))`

@export Did the regulator answer my written request for a prosecution in time (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) s 231 (2))?
GIVEN `received on` IS A DATE @desc The day the regulator received your request
      `advised` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the regulator has advised you in writing
      `advised on` IS A DATE @desc The day it advised you (if it has not, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (used when it has not advised you)
GIVETH A `The regulator's answer`
`s 231 (2) -- did the regulator answer in time` `received on` `advised` `advised on` `today` MEANS
    IF `advised`
    THEN IF `advice on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day advised`
         THEN `in time: the regulator advised you in writing within 3 months after receiving your request (s 231 (2))`
         ELSE `late: the regulator did not advise you in writing within 3 months after receiving your request (s 231 (2))`
    ELSE IF `the last day` `falls before` `today`
         THEN `late: the regulator did not advise you in writing within 3 months after receiving your request (s 231 (2))`
         ELSE `not yet advised, and the 3 months have not run out (s 231 (2))`
    WHERE
        `the last day` MEANS 3 `months after` `received on`
        `the days allowed` MEANS `the days from` `received on` `to` `the last day`
        `the raw day` MEANS `the days from` `received on` `to` `advised on`
        `the day advised` MEANS IF `the raw day` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `the raw day`

-- s 231 (3) (b): if the regulator will not refer the matter to the DPP, and "the person makes a written request to the
-- regulator to do so, refer the matter to the DPP within 1 month of the request".
@export If the regulator will not refer the matter to the DPP and I ask it in writing to do so, by when must it refer the matter (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) s 231 (3) (b))? Within 1 month of your request.
GIVEN `asked on` IS A DATE @desc The day you made your written request to refer the matter to the DPP
GIVETH A DATE
`s 231 (3) -- the last day for referral to the DPP` `asked on` MEANS 1 `months after` `asked on`

§§ `s 232 -- How long the business can be prosecuted`

-- s 232 (1): "A proceeding for an offence against this Act may be brought within the latest of the following periods:
-- (a) within 2 years after the offence first comes to the notice of the regulator; (b) within 1 year after a coronial
-- report was made or a coronial inquiry or inquest ended, if it appeared ... that an offence had been committed ...; (c)
-- if a WHS undertaking has been given in relation to the offence -- within 6 months after (i) the WHS undertaking is
-- contravened; or (ii) it comes to the notice of the regulator that [it] has been contravened; or (iii) the regulator has
-- agreed ... to the withdrawal of the WHS undertaking." (2), (3): later, with the court's leave, only if in the
-- interests of justice. (4): "This section does not apply to a proceeding for an industrial manslaughter offence."
-- For (c) the person gives the latest of the three days that has happened (fork F9).
@export What is the last day on which the business or person responsible can be prosecuted under the Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) without the court's leave (s 232)? The latest of: 2 years after the offence came to the regulator's notice; 1 year after a coronial report or inquest that showed it; 6 months after a WHS undertaking about it was breached or withdrawn. There is no limit in s 232 for industrial manslaughter.
GIVEN `industrial manslaughter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offence is industrial manslaughter (s 34A)
      `noticed on` IS A DATE @desc The day the offence first came to the regulator's notice
      `coronial` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a coronial report, inquiry or inquest made it appear an offence had been committed against the Act
      `coronial on` IS A DATE @desc The day the coronial report was made or the inquiry or inquest ended (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `undertaking` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a WHS undertaking was given about the offence and has since been contravened, or its contravention came to the regulator's notice, or the regulator agreed to its withdrawal
      `undertaking on` IS A DATE @desc The latest of those days (otherwise any date; it is not used)
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 232 -- the last day for a prosecution` `industrial manslaughter` `noticed on` `coronial` `coronial on` `undertaking` `undertaking on` MEANS
    IF `industrial manslaughter`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`the later of` (`the later of` `period a` `and` `period b`) `and` `period c`)
    WHERE
        `period a` MEANS 2 `years after` `noticed on`
        `period b` MEANS IF `coronial` THEN 1 `years after` `coronial on` ELSE `period a`
        `period c` MEANS IF `undertaking` THEN 6 `months after` `undertaking on` ELSE `period a`

DECLARE `Can they still be prosecuted` IS ONE OF
    `yes: within the limitation period (s 232 (1))`
    `yes, with the court's leave, which it gives because it is in the interests of justice (s 232 (2), (3))`
    `no: the limitation period has ended and the court will not give leave, not being satisfied it is in the interests of justice (s 232 (3)) (the text's no)`
    `s 232 does not apply to industrial manslaughter; this Act sets no time limit for it (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Can the business or person responsible still be prosecuted, on a given day, under the Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) (s 232)?
GIVEN `industrial manslaughter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offence is industrial manslaughter (s 34A)
      `noticed on` IS A DATE @desc The day the offence first came to the regulator's notice
      `coronial` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a coronial report, inquiry or inquest made it appear an offence had been committed against the Act
      `coronial on` IS A DATE @desc The day the coronial report was made or the inquiry or inquest ended (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `undertaking` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a WHS undertaking was given about the offence and has since been contravened, or its contravention came to the regulator's notice, or the regulator agreed to its withdrawal
      `undertaking on` IS A DATE @desc The latest of those days (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `brought on` IS A DATE @desc The day the proceeding is (or would be) brought
      `leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that it is in the interests of justice to give leave to bring it late (answer as the court would find)
GIVETH A `Can they still be prosecuted`
`s 232 -- can they still be prosecuted` `industrial manslaughter` `noticed on` `coronial` `coronial on` `undertaking` `undertaking on` `brought on` `leave` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 232 -- the last day for a prosecution` `industrial manslaughter` `noticed on` `coronial` `coronial on` `undertaking` `undertaking on`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN `s 232 does not apply to industrial manslaughter; this Act sets no time limit for it (NOTHING-kind)`
    WHEN JUST `the last day` THEN
        IF NOT (`the last day` `falls before` `brought on`)
        THEN `yes: within the limitation period (s 232 (1))`
        ELSE IF `leave`
             THEN `yes, with the court's leave, which it gives because it is in the interests of justice (s 232 (2), (3))`
             ELSE `no: the limitation period has ended and the court will not give leave, not being satisfied it is in the interests of justice (s 232 (3)) (the text's no)`

§§ `s 34A -- Industrial manslaughter (this group arises only if a worker or another person died)`

-- s 34A (1): "A person commits an offence if (a) the person conducts a business or undertaking, or is an officer of a
-- person who conducts a business or undertaking; and (b) the person has a health and safety duty; and (c) the person
-- engages in conduct; and (d) the conduct results in a breach of the health and safety duty; and (e) the conduct causes
-- (i) the death of a worker; or (ii) an injury to a worker and the injury later causes the death of the worker; or (iii)
-- the death of another person; and (f) the person is reckless or negligent about causing the death ..." (4): "causes
-- death -- a person's conduct causes death if the conduct substantially contributes to the death"; "health and safety
-- duty" means a duty under division 2.2, division 2.3 or s 27 (the duties themselves are not encoded: asked).
DECLARE `Who died` IS ONE OF
    `a worker died`
    `a worker was injured and later died of the injury`
    `another person died`
    `no one died`

DECLARE `Industrial manslaughter` IS ONE OF
    `on these facts the elements of industrial manslaughter are made out (s 34A (1)); maximum penalty 20 years imprisonment for an individual, $18 000 000 for a body corporate`
    `not industrial manslaughter on these facts (the text's no: an element of s 34A (1) is missing); a category 1 or 2 offence may still be found instead (s 34B)`

@export Could the death be industrial manslaughter under the Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) (s 34A)? A business or its officer who has a health and safety duty commits industrial manslaughter if conduct breaching the duty causes the death of a worker or another person, and they were reckless or negligent about causing it.
GIVEN `conducts or officer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person said to be responsible conducts a business or undertaking, or is an officer of one (a director or someone who makes or takes part in its major decisions)
      `has the duty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they had the primary duty of care, a further duty of a business, or an officer's duty of due diligence (Divisions 2.2, 2.3 or s 27)
      `breach` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether their conduct (an act or an omission) resulted in a breach of that duty
      `who died` IS A `Who died` @desc Who died
      `substantially contributed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct substantially contributed to the death (or to the injury that later caused it)
      `reckless or negligent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they were reckless or negligent about causing the death by the conduct (answer as the court would find)
GIVETH AN `Industrial manslaughter`
`s 34A -- could it be industrial manslaughter` `conducts or officer` `has the duty` `breach` `who died` `substantially contributed` `reckless or negligent` MEANS
    IF      `conducts or officer`
        AND `has the duty`
        AND `breach`
        AND (NOT (`who died` EQUALS `no one died`))
        AND `substantially contributed`
        AND `reckless or negligent`
    THEN `on these facts the elements of industrial manslaughter are made out (s 34A (1)); maximum penalty 20 years imprisonment for an individual, $18 000 000 for a body corporate`
    ELSE `not industrial manslaughter on these facts (the text's no: an element of s 34A (1) is missing); a category 1 or 2 offence may still be found instead (s 34B)`

§§ `Schedule 4 -- The most the court can impose`

DECLARE `The offence` IS ONE OF
    `industrial manslaughter (s 34A)`
    `category 1: reckless or negligent exposure to a risk of death or serious injury (s 31)`
    `category 2: failure to comply with a health and safety duty exposing someone to a risk of death or serious injury (s 32)`
    `category 3: failure to comply with a health and safety duty (s 33)`
    `discriminatory conduct, or requesting or assisting it, coercion or misrepresentation (ss 104, 107, 108, 109: tier A)`
    `failing to notify the regulator of a notifiable incident, or to preserve the incident site (ss 38 (1), 39: tier D)`
    `charging a worker for anything done or provided for work health and safety (s 273: tier F)`

DECLARE `The offender` IS ONE OF
    `an individual who conducts the business or undertaking, or is an officer of it`
    `another individual (for example a worker)`
    `a body corporate`

-- Schedule 4 table 4.1 (categories 1 to 3: column 3 individual conducting a business or officer, column 4 other
-- individual, column 5 body corporate), s 4.1A ($18 000 000), table 4.2 (tiers: column 3 individual, column 4 body
-- corporate). The amounts before indexation.
GIVEN `the offence` IS A `The offence`, `the offender` IS A `The offender`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the Schedule 4 amount` `the offence` `the offender` MEANS
    IF `the offence` EQUALS `industrial manslaughter (s 34A)`
    THEN 18000000
    ELSE IF `the offence` EQUALS `category 1: reckless or negligent exposure to a risk of death or serious injury (s 31)`
         THEN `by offender` 2085000 1042000 10425000
         ELSE IF `the offence` EQUALS `category 2: failure to comply with a health and safety duty exposing someone to a risk of death or serious injury (s 32)`
              THEN `by offender` 418000 209000 2090000
              ELSE IF `the offence` EQUALS `category 3: failure to comply with a health and safety duty (s 33)`
                   THEN `by offender` 140000 70000 700000
                   ELSE IF `the offence` EQUALS `discriminatory conduct, or requesting or assisting it, coercion or misrepresentation (ss 104, 107, 108, 109: tier A)`
                        THEN `by offender` 139000 139000 695000
                        ELSE IF `the offence` EQUALS `failing to notify the regulator of a notifiable incident, or to preserve the incident site (ss 38 (1), 39: tier D)`
                             THEN `by offender` 14000 14000 70000
                             ELSE `by offender` 7000 7000 35000
    WHERE
        GIVEN `column 3` IS A NUMBER, `column 4` IS A NUMBER, `column 5` IS A NUMBER
        GIVETH A NUMBER
        `by offender` `column 3` `column 4` `column 5` MEANS
            IF `the offender` EQUALS `a body corporate`
            THEN `column 5`
            ELSE IF `the offender` EQUALS `an individual who conducts the business or undertaking, or is an officer of it`
                 THEN `column 3`
                 ELSE `column 4`

-- Schedule 4 s 4.5: "(a) less than $10 000 and not a multiple of $100 -- ... rounded to the nearest $100; and (ii) an
-- amount of $50 is rounded down; or (b) more than $10 000 and not a multiple of $1 000 -- ... rounded to the nearest
-- $1 000; and (ii) an amount of $500 is rounded down."
GIVEN `the unit` IS A NUMBER, `the amount` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`rounded to the nearest` `the unit` `halves down` `the amount` MEANS
    IF (`the amount` MINUS (`the units below` TIMES `the unit`)) GREATER THAN (`the unit` DIVIDED BY 2)
    THEN (`the units below` PLUS 1) TIMES `the unit`
    ELSE `the units below` TIMES `the unit`
    WHERE
        `the units below` MEANS FLOOR (`the amount` DIVIDED BY `the unit`)

GIVEN `the amount` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`rounded under s 4.5` `the amount` MEANS
    IF `the amount` LESS THAN 10000
    THEN `rounded to the nearest` 100 `halves down` `the amount`
    ELSE IF `the amount` GREATER THAN 10000
         THEN `rounded to the nearest` 1000 `halves down` `the amount`
         ELSE `the amount`

-- Schedule 4 s 4.4: the amounts in ss 4.1, 4.2 and 4.3 are indexed "for the year starting on 1 July 2024, and for each
-- subsequent year": A x B / C, B the CPI number for the March quarter in the year immediately preceding the year, C the
-- CPI number for the March quarter of 2022. s 4.1A (industrial manslaughter) is not in the list: not indexed (finding
-- 4). s 4.4 (3) (an amount lower than the previous year's: the previous year's continues) is not applied (fork F10).
-- Before 1 July 2024: these tables are not in this republication's history as deposited (NOTHING-kind).
-- An individual convicted of industrial manslaughter faces imprisonment only; no monetary penalty is stated (NOTHING-kind).
@export What is the most the court can fine the business or person responsible for an offence under the Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) (s 31-34A, ss 104-109, s 38, s 273 and Schedule 4)? The Schedule 4 amounts are indexed each year from 1 July 2024 by the March-quarter consumer price index and rounded; industrial manslaughter's $18 000 000 is not indexed.
GIVEN `the offence` IS A `The offence` @desc Which offence
      `the offender` IS A `The offender` @desc Who committed it
      `year starting` IS A NUMBER @desc The calendar year in which the 12 months starting on 1 July begin, for the year whose amount you want (for example 2025 for 1 July 2025 to 30 June 2026)
      `CPI B` IS A NUMBER @desc The All Groups Consumer Price Index number (weighted average of the 8 capital cities) for the March quarter of that same calendar year, as published by the Australian Statistician
      `CPI C` IS A NUMBER @desc The same index number for the March quarter of 2022
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`Schedule 4 -- the most the court can fine` `the offence` `the offender` `year starting` `CPI B` `CPI C` MEANS
    IF `year starting` LESS THAN 2024
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `the offence` EQUALS `industrial manslaughter (s 34A)`
         THEN IF `the offender` EQUALS `a body corporate` THEN JUST 18000000 ELSE NOTHING
         ELSE JUST (`rounded under s 4.5` ((`the Schedule 4 amount` `the offence` `the offender` TIMES `CPI B`) DIVIDED BY `CPI C`))

-- s 31 (penalty): an individual, "category 1 monetary penalty or imprisonment for 10 years or both"; s 34A (penalty):
-- an individual, "imprisonment for 20 years". The other offences listed carry no imprisonment.
@export What is the longest imprisonment an individual can be given for an offence under the Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) (s 31, s 34A)? In years; 0 where the offence carries no imprisonment, and 0 for a body corporate.
GIVEN `the offence` IS A `The offence` @desc Which offence
      `the offender` IS A `The offender` @desc Who committed it
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the longest imprisonment in years` `the offence` `the offender` MEANS
    IF `the offender` EQUALS `a body corporate`
    THEN 0
    ELSE IF `the offence` EQUALS `industrial manslaughter (s 34A)`
         THEN 20
         ELSE IF `the offence` EQUALS `category 1: reckless or negligent exposure to a risk of death or serious injury (s 31)`
              THEN 10
              ELSE 0
